        S(
            "The main methodologies explained",
            H3("Waterfall"),
            "Waterfall moves through fixed phases, typically requirements, design, build, test and deployment, each signed off before the next starts. Winston Royce described this sequence in a 1970 paper, while also warning that a purely sequential process is risky. It works well when requirements are clear and changes are expensive, as in construction or hardware. The risk is that problems surface late, at testing or handover. Read more in our guide to [Waterfall project management](/project-management/waterfall-project-management/).",
            H3("Agile"),
            "Agile is an approach based on the values and principles of the Agile Manifesto (2001): deliver working results in short cycles, collaborate closely with customers and respond to change. It is not one method but a family that includes Scrum, Kanban and XP. It suits work where requirements are uncertain and early feedback is valuable. See our guide to the [Agile methodology](/project-management/agile/agile-methodology/) and the [types of Agile methodologies](/project-management/agile/types-of-agile-methodologies/).",
            H3("Scrum"),
            "Scrum is the most widely known Agile framework. Under the 2020 Scrum Guide, a Scrum Team of usually ten or fewer people (a Product Owner, a Scrum Master and Developers) works in sprints of one month or less, with five events (the Sprint, Sprint Planning, Daily Scrum, Sprint Review and Sprint Retrospective) and three artifacts (Product Backlog, Sprint Backlog and Increment). It suits product development where a usable increment can be produced every sprint. See [what Scrum is](/project-management/scrum/what-is-scrum/).",
            H3("Kanban"),
            "Kanban manages work as a continuous flow. Work items move across a visual board, work-in-progress (WIP) limits stop the team starting more than it can finish, and flow metrics such as cycle time and throughput drive improvement. There are no prescribed roles or sprints, so it fits support, operations, marketing and any team with a steady stream of requests. See [what Kanban is](/project-management/kanban/what-is-kanban/).",
            H3("Scrumban"),
            "Scrumban combines Scrum's planning and review cadence with Kanban's flow and WIP limits. It is popular with teams that handle planned feature work alongside unplanned requests, or teams moving from Scrum to Kanban. Compare the options in [Kanban vs Scrumban](/project-management/kanban/kanban-vs-scrumban/).",
            H3("Lean"),
            "Lean comes from the Toyota Production System. It focuses on delivering customer value with the least waste, using ideas such as value stream mapping, pull and continuous improvement. In projects it is used in construction (for example the Last Planner System), operations and alongside Agile. See [Lean project management](/project-management/lean-project-management/).",
            H3("Six Sigma"),
            "Six Sigma is a data-driven method for reducing defects and variation, run as projects that follow the DMAIC cycle (Define, Measure, Analyse, Improve, Control). It is strongest on repeatable processes with measurable output and is often combined with Lean as Lean Six Sigma. See our [Six Sigma guide](/project-management/six-sigma/).",
            H3("PRINCE2"),
            "PRINCE2 (PRojects IN Controlled Environments) is a process-based method that originated with the UK government and is now owned by PeopleCert, which launched the current version, PRINCE2 7, in October 2023. It has seven principles, seven practices and seven processes. Projects are divided into management stages, a project board makes key decisions, a business case must remain valid throughout, and the project manager works within agreed tolerances (management by exception). See our [PRINCE2 guide](/project-management/prince2/).",
            H3("Critical path method"),
            "The critical path method, developed in the late 1950s, is a scheduling technique rather than a full methodology. You list activities, durations and dependencies, then find the longest chain of dependent tasks, which sets the earliest finish date. It is used inside predictive and hybrid projects. See our guide to the [critical path method](/project-management/critical-path-method/).",
            H3("Critical chain project management"),
            "Critical chain, developed by Eliyahu Goldratt from his theory of constraints, schedules around resource availability as well as dependencies. Safety time is removed from individual tasks and pooled into shared buffers, and progress is tracked by buffer consumption. It suits organisations where scarce specialists are shared across projects. See [critical chain project management](/project-management/critical-chain-project-management/).",
            H3("Extreme Programming (XP)"),
            "XP is an Agile software development method created by Kent Beck in the late 1990s. It centres on engineering practices such as pair programming, test-driven development, continuous integration and small, frequent releases. Many teams use XP practices inside Scrum or Kanban.",
            H3("Extreme project management (XPM)"),
            "Extreme project management, described by Doug DeCarlo in 2004, is for projects where the goal, the solution or both are unclear and conditions change quickly, such as innovation and R&D. It works in rapid cycles and treats the plan as a hypothesis. See [extreme project management](/project-management/extreme-project-management/).",
            H3("Scaled Agile frameworks"),
            "When many Agile teams work on one product, scaling frameworks such as SAFe, LeSS and Scrum@Scale add coordination: shared planning events, roles across teams and portfolio management. See our guide to the [Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe)](/project-management/agile/scaled-agile-framework-safe/).",
        ),
